{"timestamp": "2026-01-16 03:20:39", "level": "DEBUG", "module": "anthropic._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d5b60d0f-dd6f-4697-bf20-2dcb1314a356', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4096, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Please complete the following task:\n\n{\n  "requirements_file": "src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md",\n  "project_name": "agent-cost-tracking-enhancement",\n  "output_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md"\n}\n\nRemember to respond with valid JSON matching the expected output format.\n'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_014dQsQtNFz7KWnnVHKpT9Mh', 'name': 'read_file', 'input': {'file_path': 'src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_014dQsQtNFz7KWnnVHKpT9Mh', 'content': '{"success": true, "content": "# Requirements: Agent Cost Tracking Enhancement\\n\\n## Project Vision\\nEnhance the ensemble UI to display cost estimates, execution duration, and model information for each agent task, enabling users to understand the resource consumption and performance characteristics of their AI workflows.\\n\\n## Problem Statement\\nCurrently, the ensemble UI shows agent execution status and basic information, but provides no visibility into:\\n- Cost incurred by each agent\'s API usage\\n- Time taken for agent execution\\n- Which AI model was used\\n- Token consumption patterns\\n\\nThis lack of visibility makes it difficult for users to optimize workflows, understand resource consumption, or budget for AI operations.\\n\\n## Solution Overview\\nAdd comprehensive cost and performance tracking to the existing activity tracking and display pipeline:\\n\\n1. **Backend Enhancement**: Extend activity tracking to capture timing, cost, and model usage\\n2. **Cost Calculation**: Implement cost calculator with current model pricing\\n3. **API Extension**: Include new metrics in existing endpoints\\n4. **UI Enhancement**: Display cost, duration, and model badges in agent summary panes\\n\\n## Objectives\\n\\n### Primary Objectives\\n1. **Display Agent Costs**: Show cost estimate for each completed agent\\n2. **Show Execution Duration**: Display formatted execution time for each agent\\n3. **Display Model Information**: Show which AI model was used per agent\\n4. **Maintain Performance**: No impact on agent execution speed\\n5. **Preserve Compatibility**: All enhancements backward compatible\\n\\n### Secondary Objectives\\n1. **Accurate Metrics**: Use actual token usage when available\\n2. **Graceful Degradation**: Handle missing data appropriately\\n3. **Clear Display**: Intuitive formatting of cost and duration\\n4. **Real-time Updates**: Metrics appear as soon as agent completes\\n\\n## Functional Requirements\\n\\n### FR1: Cost Tracking\\n- **FR1.1**: Calculate cost based on actual token usage from Claude API\\n- **FR1.2**: Support Claude 3.5 Sonnet, Opus, and Haiku models\\n- **FR1.3**: Display cost in USD with appropriate precision\\n- **FR1.4**: Handle cases where token usage unavailable (estimation fallback)\\n\\n### FR2: Duration Tracking\\n- **FR2.1**: Track agent start and completion times\\n- **FR2.2**: Calculate duration in milliseconds\\n- **FR2.3**: Display duration with appropriate units (ms, s, m s)\\n- **FR2.4**: Show execution timeline in agent summary\\n\\n### FR3: Model Information\\n- **FR3.1**: Capture model identifier from agent runtime\\n- **FR3.2**: Display shortened model name (e.g., \\"Sonnet 3.5\\")\\n- **FR3.3**: Associate model with specific agent execution\\n\\n### FR4: Data Integration\\n- **FR4.1**: Extend activity tracker to capture new metrics\\n- **FR4.2**: Include metrics in WebSocket/REST API responses\\n- **FR4.3**: Update agent hierarchy data structure\\n- **FR4.4**: Maintain existing API contract (new fields optional)\\n\\n### FR5: UI Display\\n- **FR5.1**: Add cost badge to AgentSummaryPane\\n- **FR5.2**: Add duration badge to AgentSummaryPane\\n- **FR5.3**: Add model tag to AgentSummaryPane\\n- **FR5.4**: Show start/completion times\\n- **FR5.5**: Handle missing metrics gracefully\\n\\n## Technical Requirements\\n\\n### TR1: Backend Components\\n- **TR1.1**: Create `CostCalculator` class with model pricing table\\n- **TR1.2**: Extend `ActivityTracker` to capture timing and cost data\\n- **TR1.3**: Integrate with `AgentRuntime` to extract metrics\\n- **TR1.4**: Update API endpoints to include new fields\\n\\n### TR2: Data Structure\\n```python\\n# Enhanced agent data structure\\n{\\n    \\"agent_id\\": str,\\n    \\"agent_name\\": str,\\n    \\"agent_type\\": str,\\n    \\"status\\": str,\\n    \\"started_at\\": str,  # ISO timestamp\\n    \\"completed_at\\": str,  # ISO timestamp (optional)\\n    \\"duration_ms\\": int,  # milliseconds (optional)\\n    \\"model_used\\": str,  # e.g., \\"claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022\\"\\n    \\"cost_estimate\\": float,  # USD (optional)\\n    \\"token_usage\\": {  # optional\\n        \\"input_tokens\\": int,\\n        \\"output_tokens\\": int,\\n        \\"total_tokens\\": int\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### TR3: Model Pricing\\nCurrent Claude pricing (January 2024):\\n- **Claude 3.5 Sonnet**: $3/million input, $15/million output\\n- **Claude 3 Opus**: $15/million input, $75/million output\\n- **Claude 3 Haiku**: $0.25/million input, $1.25/million output\\n\\n### TR4: Frontend Components\\n- **TR4.1**: Update `AgentSummaryPane.jsx` with new display elements\\n- **TR4.2**: Implement formatting utilities for cost/duration\\n- **TR4.3**: Add appropriate icons/styling for metrics\\n- **TR4.4**: Ensure responsive layout\\n\\n## User Stories\\n\\n### US1: Cost Visibility\\n**As a** user running AI workflows  \\n**I want to** see the cost of each agent execution  \\n**So that** I can understand and optimize my AI spending\\n\\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\\n- Cost displayed as badge in agent summary (e.g., \\"$0.0042\\")\\n- Cost calculated from actual token usage when available\\n- Cost appears when agent completes\\n- Cost shows appropriate precision (4 decimal places for small amounts)\\n\\n### US2: Performance Monitoring\\n**As a** user debugging workflows  \\n**I want to** see how long each agent takes to execute  \\n**So that** I can identify performance bottlenecks\\n\\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\\n- Duration displayed as badge in agent summary (e.g., \\"2.3s\\")\\n- Duration shows from agent start to completion\\n- Duration formatted appropriately (ms for <1s, s for <1min, m s for longer)\\n- Start/completion times visible in summary\\n\\n### US3: Model Transparency\\n**As a** user optimizing workflows  \\n**I want to** see which AI model each agent used  \\n**So that** I can understand model selection and costs\\n\\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\\n- Model displayed as tag in agent summary (e.g., \\"Sonnet 3.5\\")\\n- Model name shortened for readability\\n- Model associated with specific agent execution\\n- Model information persists after completion\\n\\n### US4: Real-time Updates\\n**As a** user monitoring live workflows  \\n**I want to** see metrics appear as agents complete  \\n**So that** I can track progress and costs in real-time\\n\\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\\n- Start time appears when agent begins\\n- Metrics appear when agent completes\\n- UI updates via WebSocket without page refresh\\n- No visible delays in metric display\\n\\n## Constraints\\n\\n### Technical Constraints\\n- **TC1**: Must not impact agent execution performance\\n- **TC2**: Must maintain backward compatibility with existing API consumers\\n- **TC3**: Must handle network failures gracefully during metric collection\\n- **TC4**: Must use existing authentication and authorization mechanisms\\n\\n### Business Constraints\\n- **BC1**: Model pricing hardcoded initially (updatable in future)\\n- **BC2**: No budget controls or alerting in initial version\\n- **BC3**: No historical analytics or cost aggregation\\n- **BC4**: Focus on individual agent metrics only\\n\\n### Technology Constraints\\n- **TK1**: Use existing Python backend and React frontend\\n- **TK2**: Leverage existing activity tracking infrastructure\\n- **TK3**: Follow existing UI design patterns and styling\\n- **TK4**: Use Claude API token usage data as primary source\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n\\n### User Acceptance\\n1. **Cost Visibility**: Users can see cost for 95% of completed agents\\n2. **Duration Accuracy**: Duration displayed within 100ms of actual execution time\\n3. **Model Information**: Model name visible for all agents\\n4. **Performance Impact**: < 5% overhead added to agent execution time\\n5. **UI Responsiveness**: Metrics appear within 1 second of agent completion\\n\\n### Technical Acceptance\\n1. **API Compatibility**: Existing API consumers continue working without changes\\n2. **Error Handling**: System gracefully handles missing or invalid metric data\\n3. **Test Coverage**: 90% test coverage for new components\\n4. **Documentation**: All new APIs and components documented\\n\\n## Out of Scope\\n\\n### Current Version\\n- **Historical cost analytics and reporting**\\n- **Budget controls and alerting**\\n- **Cost breakdown by tool or iteration**\\n- **Real-time streaming cost updates during execution**\\n- **Cost aggregation across multiple agents**\\n- **External cost tracking integrations**\\n- **Cost optimization recommendations**\\n\\n### Future Considerations\\n- Integration with external budget tracking systems\\n- Advanced cost analytics and visualizations\\n- Predictive cost modeling\\n- Multi-cloud cost tracking\\n- Cost alerts and budget controls\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n### Technical Assumptions\\n- Claude API responses include `usage` field with token counts\\n- Activity tracker can be extended without breaking existing functionality\\n- WebSocket connections remain stable for real-time updates\\n- Agent runtime provides access to model configuration\\n\\n### Business Assumptions\\n- Current model pricing remains stable during implementation\\n- Users primarily interested in per-agent costs vs. aggregate costs\\n- Cost precision to 4 decimal places sufficient for user needs\\n- Duration tracking in milliseconds adequate for performance analysis\\n\\n### User Assumptions\\n- Users understand AI model cost concepts\\n- Users can interpret duration and cost metrics effectively\\n- Users will use cost information for workflow optimization\\n- Users prioritize accuracy over real-time streaming updates\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n### Internal Dependencies\\n- Activity Tracker system (`activity_tracker.py`)\\n- Agent Runtime system\\n- WebSocket/API infrastructure (`main.py`)\\n- AgentSummaryPane component\\n- Existing authentication/authorization\\n\\n### External Dependencies\\n- Claude API with consistent token usage reporting\\n- React framework for frontend updates\\n- Python 3.11+ for backend enhancements\\n- Existing build and deployment pipeline\\n\\n## Implementation Approach\\n\\n### Development Methodology\\n- **Test-Driven Development (TDD)**: Write tests first, then implementation\\n- **Incremental Delivery**: Backend \\u2192 API \\u2192 Frontend phases\\n- **Backward Compatibility**: Ensure existing functionality unaffected\\n- **Performance Monitoring**: Track impact on execution speed\\n\\n### Testing Strategy\\n1. **Unit Tests**: Cost calculation, formatting utilities, data structures\\n2. **Integration Tests**: End-to-end flow from agent execution to UI display\\n3. **API Tests**: Response format validation and backward compatibility\\n4. **Frontend Tests**: Component rendering and data handling\\n5. **Performance Tests**: Execution overhead measurement\\n\\n### Risk Mitigation\\n- **Missing Token Data**: Fallback to estimation based on character counts\\n- **API Changes**: Defensive programming for token usage field changes\\n- **Performance Impact**: Asynchronous processing where possible\\n- **UI Compatibility**: Progressive enhancement with feature detection\\n\\nThis requirements document provides the foundation for implementing agent cost tracking enhancement using test-driven development methodology."}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-3-5-haiku-20241022', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 96.6%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.5\n- Common errors to avoid: RateLimitError, CircuitBreakerOpenError\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Watch out for these common errors: RateLimitError, CircuitBreakerOpenError\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- The architecture proposal successfully captures the project requirements by providing a detailed, extensible design that balances technical depth with practical implementation strategies. The approach...\n- The architecture proposal effectively addresses the three key improvements by providing a modular, event-driven design that enhances system observability and resilience. The approach balances technica...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branch_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the branch (for create, switch, delete)'}, 'from_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Base branch to create from (for create, defaults to current branch)'}, 'force': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Force the operation (for delete unmerged branches)', 'default': False}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_merge', 'description': 'Merge branches or create pull requests for code review', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'preview', 'abort'], 'description': 'Action: merge branches, preview changes, or abort an in-progress merge'}, 'source_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge from'}, 'target_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge into (defaults to current branch)'}, 'strategy': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'squash', 'rebase'], 'description': 'Merge strategy (default: merge)', 'default': 'merge'}, 'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Custom merge commit message (optional)'}, 'no_ff': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Create merge commit even if fast-forward is possible', 'default': True}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_status', 'description': 'Get comprehensive git repository status including branches, changes, and history', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'include_history': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Include recent commit history', 'default': False}, 'history_count': {'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Number of commits to include in history', 'default': 10}}, 'required': []}}, {'name': 'project_tracking', 'description': 'Manage project state, tasks, and notes for the current project', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create_project', 'add_task', 'update_task', 'add_note', 'get_summary', 'get_next_tasks'], 'description': 'Action to perform'}, 'project_id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project ID (not needed for create_project)'}, 'project_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project name (for create_project)'}, 'description': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Description (for create_project or add_task)'}, 'task_id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Task ID (for update_task)'}, 'status': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['todo', 'in_progress', 'completed', 'blocked', 'cancelled'], 'description': 'Task status (for update_task)'}, 'note': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Note text (for add_note or update_task)'}, 'assigned_to': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Agent type (for add_task)'}, 'category': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['general', 'decision', 'milestone', 'issue'], 'description': 'Note category (for add_note)'}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'spawn_agent', 'description': 'Spawn and execute a specialist agent to perform a specific task', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'agent_type': {'type': 'string', 'description': "The type of agent to spawn (e.g., 'code_writer', 'code_tester')"}, 'input_data': {'type': 'object', 'description': 'Input data to pass to the spawned agent'}}, 'required': ['agent_type', 'input_data']}}]}}"}
